I don't think there's much to celebrate  we are paralyzed in Parliament not just  within the Conservative Party but  generally because the realization is  dawning that there is no form of brexit  which is going to be of advantage to our  country now the only people who think  that's not the case are some of my AG  colleagues which is why they willing to  contemplate a No Deal brexit but there's  a vast majority of members of parliament  who think a No Deal brexit would be  disastrous for us and as a consequence I  there's no doubt that things are getting  more tense and we've got to try to find  a way through this crisis in my view the  Prime Minister should be doing two  things you should be firstly seeking the  extension to article 50 because we're  clearly going to need more time to  debate options and secondly something  I've long believed I think we're going  to have to go back to the public and ask  the public what it is they want we can  offer them some forms of brexit they may  not be forms of brexit that I want but  nevertheless if that is what they want  we can give it to them equally if people  wish to change their views and wish to  remain in the EU it is still possible to  do that and I think we should be  thinking very carefully along those  lines if you want to find a way through  this do you think it is likely that  there could be a second referendum  because it seems as though it's less  likely than ever and a lot of people  saying you know what it may just  continue to drag this out this period of  uncertainty  I disagree I think the possibility of a  second referendum has always been there  it's apparently Labour Party policy but  it would be nice of Jeremy Corbyn were  to actually speak out for one rather  than prevaricated over it and it offers  a potential solution none of the other  things a moment appear to offer a  solution at all and that's what troubles  me clearly if there was a coming  together at the House of Commons around  a particular form of brexit then we can  exit on that particular form but  everything I've seen in the last few  months suggests that four perfectly  valid reasons opinion in the house is  deeply divided because most MPs look at  brexit and see that in fact leaving is  going to be greatly to our disadvantage  so to agree something which you know is  going to do the country damage is very  difficult as I say my ERG colleagues are  the exception to that they are  enthusiastic about leaving and they  appear to be entirely untroubled about  all the potential consequences that most  people believe will flow from No Deal  departure which I think will be chaos  and real damage to our country's  economic future and well-being and I'm  just not prepared to go there under any  circumstances I mean we've just had one  of your ERG colleagues on the programme  it's a burner Jenkins and he said that  this is actually down to the fact that  three-quarters of MPs have always been  remainders remain remain as and just  can't get over the results of the  referendum that that is a perfectly  reasonable comment but what he is asking  those three quarters of MPs to do is to  abandon their conscience their judgment  and their views and to sign up to  something which they believe will be  disastrous for their constituents it's  just not going to happen  and yet whilst all this arguing is going  on and it seems as you say to coin a  phrase we're struggling to square a  circle it looks doesn't it as though No  Deal is becoming increasingly likely 42  days to go and that's very clearly in  our sights now  but that's why I think the Prime  Minister should be taking steps to  extend the article 50 period now there's  an opportunity for her to do this but if  she doesn't do it then as was explained  by my colleagues Oliver let win yes in  the debate yesterday the House of  Commons is going to have to take action  to try to ensure that an extension can  be secured because so many of us take  the view that leaving without a deal  would be uniquely damaging to our  country and is there any deal that you  would accept I mean how would you feel  about being part of this customs union  as Jeremy Corbyn has said you know could  you tolerate that or would you see that  as a sort of soft brexit and therefore  pointless as Tony Blair has argued there  are a series of possible ways in which  we could leave the EU but I have to say  I've also taken the fundamental view a  long time ago that they should be put to  the electorate one of the problems we  have is that the debate we had in 2016  there's very little relationship to the  realities that we are now facing and  it's the fact that that's the case which  makes me so clear in my mind that we  should go back and ask the public if the  public want to leave on a Norway basis  if they want to leave on the Prime  Minister's deal which may well be the  best deal on offer and the only deal  then so be it  I will accept it but to sign something  off which has become increasingly clear  is going to do our country harm  difficult as a matter of conscience for  any member of parliament to do such a  thing
 

